# StudyHub AI — React Native (Expo) App

A real React Native app built with Expo, matching the Figma high-fidelity wireframes.
This is a **different codebase** from the earlier HTML/CSS/JS version — this one is a
proper mobile app you run through Expo Go, not a browser.

## Requirements
- [Node.js](https://nodejs.org) installed (LTS version)
- The free **Expo Go** app installed on your phone (App Store / Google Play)
- Your phone and computer on the **same Wi-Fi network**

## How to run it

1. Unzip this folder and open a terminal inside it.
2. Install dependencies (only needed once):
   ```
   npm install
   ```
3. Start the Expo development server:
   ```
   npx expo start
   ```
4. A QR code will appear in the terminal (and a browser tab).
   - **iPhone:** open the Camera app and point it at the QR code, tap the
     notification that appears.
   - **Android:** open the **Expo Go** app and tap "Scan QR code."
5. The app will build and load on your phone — start from the Splash screen.

If you want to test in a simulator/emulator instead of a real phone:
```
npx expo start --ios       (Mac only, needs Xcode)
npx expo start --android   (needs Android Studio)
```

## Project structure
```
studyhub-expo/
├── App.js                     # Shared mobile screen components
├── app.json                   # Expo app config (name, icon, splash colour)
├── package.json
├── src/
│   ├── app/                    # Expo Router routes and tab navigation
│   ├── context/                # Shared in-session task and document state
│   └── navigation.js           # Named route navigation helper
```

## How navigation works
Expo Router maps files in `src/app/` to screens. The home, files, tasks,
timetable, and chat screens use a native tab navigator; upload, summary, profile,
and task editor are separate stack screens. Task and selected-document state is
shared through `StudyHubContext` while the app is open.

The file picker adds a selected PDF to the in-app library for the current
session. This prototype does not upload files to a server, persist documents
after restart, authenticate accounts, or call a live AI service. Sample summary
and chat responses are clearly identified as examples.

## Team workflow (per assignment brief)
- Push this whole folder to your **public** GitHub repo (no private repos).
- Each member can safely work on a separate route under `src/app/` on their
  own branch, then open a Pull Request — screens don't depend on each other's
  internals, only on `navigate` and the shared `tasks` state.
- Everyone needs to run `npm install` once after pulling the repo, since
  `node_modules` should NOT be committed to Git (add a `.gitignore`, see below).

